# Frappe core concepts

Frappe is a **metadata-driven full-stack framework**: you describe your models
declaratively and the framework generates the database schema, the admin UI,
the REST API and the permission system from that description. This page is a
guided tour of the concepts, and **every concept points at the file in
`apps/ticketing/` that demonstrates it** — read this page with the code open.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph bench [bench container]
        F[Frappe Framework]{{#if withHelpdesk}} --> H[helpdesk app]{{/if}}
        F --> T[ticketing app<br/>apps/ticketing]
    end
    B[Browser] -->|/app · /helpdesk · /ticket_stats · /api| F
    F --> M[(MariaDB)]
    F --> R[(Redis cache + queue)]
```

---

## Bench, site, app

Frappe's three nested units:

- **Bench** — a self-contained installation: a Python virtualenv, the Frappe
  source, every installed app, and the process manager (`bench start`). In this
  project the bench lives *inside* the `bench` container (persisted in the
  `bench_home` Docker volume) and is created on first boot by
  `docker/init.sh`. `dude shell` drops you straight into it.
- **Site** — one tenant: one database, one set of installed apps, one config.
  A bench can host many sites; this project provisions one, named after
  `SITE_NAME` in `.env` (default `{{projectName}}.localhost` — Frappe routes
  requests to sites by hostname, which is why the URL matters).
- **App** — a Python package that plugs into the framework: models (DocTypes),
  server code, web pages, hooks. `frappe` itself is an app{{#if withHelpdesk}}; so is
  `helpdesk`{{/if}}; so is our custom **`ticketing`** app in `apps/ticketing/`.

Custom apps under `apps/` in this repo are mounted into the container and
installed in **editable mode** (`docker/init.sh`), so editing a file on the
host reloads the dev server live.

