## hooks.py — the app's declarative contract

`apps/ticketing/ticketing/hooks.py` is the single place where the app tells
the framework what it provides. Keep it declarative; put logic in dedicated
modules. The example declares:

- **`doc_events`** — run code when documents of *any* app change. The example
  hooks {{#if withHelpdesk}}Helpdesk's{{/if}} `HD Ticket` (`after_insert`, `on_update`) and points
  at plain functions in `apps/ticketing/ticketing/events/hd_ticket.py`. This
  is the canonical way to extend another app **without forking it**.
- **`scheduler_events`** — background jobs by frequency (`all`, `hourly`,
  `daily`, `weekly`, `monthly`, `cron`). The example wires two jobs in
  `apps/ticketing/ticketing/tasks.py` (see below).
- **`fixtures`** — records (not code) the app ships: exported to
  `apps/ticketing/ticketing/fixtures/*.json` and re-imported on every
  `bench migrate`. The example ships a Workflow and its states/actions.

`dude lint` verifies the contract stays honest: every dotted path in
`hooks.py` must resolve to a real function, and declared fixtures must match
the shipped JSON files (APP rules — see `.claude/rules/APP/`).

